1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:01,760
Don't take the raise.

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:03,470
You'll take home less.

3
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:05,350
Wrong.

4
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:06,510
Mostly.

5
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:07,700
Rewind.

6
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,050
Your boss offers you

7
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:10,980
a $2,000 raise.

8
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:12,350
And your coworker leans

9
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:13,630
over and whispers:

10
00:00:13,630 --> 00:00:14,780
don't take it.

11
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:15,831
It'll push you into

12
00:00:15,831 --> 00:00:17,460
a higher tax bracket.

13
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:19,340
That advice is wrong.

14
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:20,430
But the part that's

15
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:21,671
right could cost you

16
00:00:21,671 --> 00:00:22,560
thousands.

17
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:23,520
So let's do the

18
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:24,850
actual math.

19
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:25,960
Meet Maya.

20
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:26,689
She's single,

21
00:00:26,689 --> 00:00:27,444
and she earns

22
00:00:27,444 --> 00:00:30,570
$66,500 a year.

23
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:32,170
In 2026,

24
00:00:32,170 --> 00:00:33,530
the standard deduction for

25
00:00:33,530 --> 00:00:34,710
a single filer is

26
00:00:34,710 --> 00:00:37,030
$16,100.

27
00:00:37,030 --> 00:00:38,070
Take that off,

28
00:00:38,070 --> 00:00:39,650
and Maya's taxable income

29
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:43,100
is $50,400.

30
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:43,740
Now,

31
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:45,290
picture the tax brackets

32
00:00:45,290 --> 00:00:46,360
as buckets.

33
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:47,170
Her first

34
00:00:47,170 --> 00:00:49,430
$12,400 fills a bucket

35
00:00:49,430 --> 00:00:49,960
taxed at

36
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:51,300
10%.

37
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:52,590
Everything after that,

38
00:00:52,590 --> 00:00:52,930
up to

39
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:54,375
$50,400,

40
00:00:54,375 --> 00:00:55,120
fills the

41
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:56,970
12% bucket.

42
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:57,590
And Maya's

43
00:00:57,590 --> 00:00:59,580
12% bucket is full.

44
00:00:59,580 --> 00:01:01,020
Right to the brim.

45
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:02,166
So her raise spills

46
00:01:02,166 --> 00:01:02,750
over,

47
00:01:02,750 --> 00:01:03,090
into the

48
00:01:03,090 --> 00:01:05,030
22% bucket.

49
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:06,190
This is exactly what

50
00:01:06,190 --> 00:01:07,426
her coworker was scared

51
00:01:07,426 --> 00:01:08,070
of.

52
00:01:08,070 --> 00:01:09,430
But watch what actually

53
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:10,330
happens.

54
00:01:10,330 --> 00:01:11,660
Only the overflow gets

55
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:12,140
taxed at

56
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:13,640
22%.

57
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:14,930
The money already in

58
00:01:14,930 --> 00:01:16,010
the lower buckets?

59
00:01:16,010 --> 00:01:17,270
It's taxed exactly the

60
00:01:17,270 --> 00:01:18,610
same as before.

61
00:01:18,610 --> 00:01:19,990
$2,000 times

62
00:01:19,990 --> 00:01:21,310
22% is

63
00:01:21,310 --> 00:01:23,300
$440.

64
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:23,970
Maya keeps

65
00:01:23,970 --> 00:01:26,820
$1,560 of her raise,

66
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:28,810
before payroll taxes.

67
00:01:28,810 --> 00:01:30,250
Her whole federal income

68
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:30,914
tax bill is

69
00:01:30,914 --> 00:01:33,500
$6,240.

70
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:33,900
That's about

71
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:35,530
9% of her pay.

72
00:01:35,530 --> 00:01:37,060
Not 22.

73
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:38,340
A higher bracket can't

74
00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:39,770
shrink your paycheck.

75
00:01:39,770 --> 00:01:41,420
The buckets only fill.

76
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:43,050
They never tip over.

77
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:43,860
But here's what her

78
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:45,920
coworker almost got right.

79
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:47,070
Some money rules don't

80
00:01:47,070 --> 00:01:48,390
work like buckets.

81
00:01:48,390 --> 00:01:50,140
They work like cliffs.

82
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:51,280
Meet Dan.

83
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:52,220
He's 60,

84
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:53,350
he's a freelance designer,

85
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:54,380
and he buys his

86
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:55,680
health insurance on the

87
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:57,260
ACA marketplace.

88
00:01:57,260 --> 00:01:58,250
A tax credit helps

89
00:01:58,250 --> 00:02:00,240
pay his monthly premium.

90
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:01,470
Extra help that Congress

91
00:02:01,470 --> 00:02:02,010
added in

92
00:02:02,010 --> 00:02:03,650
2021 expired at the

93
00:02:03,650 --> 00:02:04,110
end of

94
00:02:04,110 --> 00:02:05,530
2025.

95
00:02:05,530 --> 00:02:05,820
So for

96
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:07,060
2026,

97
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:07,970
the old rule is

98
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:08,850
back.

99
00:02:08,850 --> 00:02:09,646
If your income is

100
00:02:09,646 --> 00:02:11,420
above 400% of the

101
00:02:11,420 --> 00:02:12,760
federal poverty line,

102
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:14,025
that credit drops to

103
00:02:14,025 --> 00:02:14,880
zero.

104
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:16,327
Not down a little.

105
00:02:16,327 --> 00:02:17,180
Zero.

106
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:18,308
For a single person

107
00:02:18,308 --> 00:02:19,160
in the lower

108
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:20,290
48 states,

109
00:02:20,290 --> 00:02:21,020
the line is

110
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:23,260
$62,600.

111
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:24,430
Dan's income is

112
00:02:24,430 --> 00:02:26,160
$62,000.

113
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:27,400
Then a client sends

114
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:29,030
one more small job.

115
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:30,630
$1,000.

116
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:31,550
Now he's at

117
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:31,550
$63,000.

118
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:35,980
$400 over the line.

119
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:37,500
And his entire credit

120
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:38,630
is gone.

121
00:02:38,750 --> 00:02:40,060
How much is that?

122
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:40,610
For a

123
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:42,433
60-year-old in an average-cost

124
00:02:42,433 --> 00:02:43,240
area,

125
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:46,640
roughly $9,700 a year.

126
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:48,400
A $1,000 job just

127
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:49,990
cost him almost ten

128
00:02:49,990 --> 00:02:51,760
times what it paid.

129
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:53,610
So did one extra

130
00:02:53,610 --> 00:02:55,810
job make Dan poorer?

131
00:02:55,810 --> 00:02:57,010
Yes.

132
00:02:57,020 --> 00:02:58,701
Unless he knows one

133
00:02:58,701 --> 00:02:59,570
move.

134
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:00,970
The cliff isn't measured

135
00:03:00,970 --> 00:03:02,360
by his paycheck.

136
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:03,870
It's measured by something

137
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:05,590
called modified adjusted gross

138
00:03:05,590 --> 00:03:06,720
income.

139
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:08,256
And some contributions lower

140
00:03:08,256 --> 00:03:09,460
that number.

141
00:03:09,490 --> 00:03:10,550
Put money into a

142
00:03:10,550 --> 00:03:11,681
traditional IRA,

143
00:03:11,681 --> 00:03:12,560
or a health savings

144
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:13,720
account if his plan

145
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:14,270
qualifies,

146
00:03:14,270 --> 00:03:15,939
and Dan can slide

147
00:03:15,939 --> 00:03:17,720
back under the line.

148
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:19,080
He keeps the job.

149
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:20,200
He keeps the money,

150
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:20,906
saved for later.

151
00:03:20,906 --> 00:03:22,750
And he keeps his

152
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:23,750
credit.

153
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:24,950
One warning:

154
00:03:24,950 --> 00:03:26,870
this rule is moving.

155
00:03:26,870 --> 00:03:27,952
The House voted in

156
00:03:27,952 --> 00:03:29,130
January to bring the

157
00:03:29,130 --> 00:03:30,620
extra help back.

158
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:31,270
So far,

159
00:03:31,270 --> 00:03:32,810
the Senate hasn't.

160
00:03:32,810 --> 00:03:33,974
So check the current

161
00:03:33,974 --> 00:03:34,350
rules,

162
00:03:34,350 --> 00:03:35,340
or ask a tax

163
00:03:35,340 --> 00:03:35,850
pro,

164
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:36,960
before you plan around

165
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:37,950
a cliff.

166
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:39,980
Brackets are buckets.

167
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:41,118
They never punish a

168
00:03:41,118 --> 00:03:42,090
raise.

169
00:03:42,110 --> 00:03:44,000
Cliffs are different.

170
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:45,052
So the next time

171
00:03:45,052 --> 00:03:45,750
someone says,

172
00:03:45,750 --> 00:03:47,190
don't take the raise,

173
00:03:47,190 --> 00:03:48,980
ask one question.

174
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:50,080
Is it a bucket,

175
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:51,000
or a cliff?
